t(37, 550, """The time of the Rawdah is coming, God willing .. for whoever understands the meaning 😃

And what a Rawdah .. not like any Rawdah""")

t(37, 1690, """Guided aright, not infallible

It is not right for him to say: Correct me, nor is it right for him to say: Obey me as long as I obey Allah concerning you. It is only right for him to take the pledge of allegiance on obedience, without discussion or argument.

Most people in the time of his appearance will not understand this serious matter.

1. The explicit Prophetic text commanding allegiance to him and holding fast to him (even crawling over snow)

* The text: From Thawban, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: "...So when you see him, pledge allegiance to him, even if crawling over snow, for he is the Caliph of Allah, the Mahdi" (narrated by al-Hakim in al-Mustadrak, who graded it sound by the conditions of the two Shaykhs, and by Ibn Majah and Ahmad).
* Indication and particularity: The Prophet ﷺ described him as "the Caliph of Allah", a great honorific description not applied to any of the Rightly Guided Caliphs nor to any man in history (the prophets and messengers being excepted from this statement and clarification). And the command to pledge allegiance to him, and to bear the hardship of crawling over snow to reach him, cuts off any room for argument or discussion; the command here is one of adherence and absolute obedience to save the religion, not a political pledge open to review.

2. The text on his leading the Prophet of Allah Jesus son of Mary, peace be upon him, in prayer
* The text: From Jabir ibn Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I heard the Prophet ﷺ say: "...Then Jesus son of Mary ﷺ will descend, and their leader will say: Come, lead us in prayer. He will say: No, some of you are leaders over others, as an honor from Allah to this Ummah" (narrated by Muslim). In the narration of al-Harith ibn Abi Usamah: "Their leader, the Mahdi, will say: Come, lead us in prayer..."
* Indication and particularity: His stepping forward for prayer ahead of a messenger prophet such as Jesus, peace be upon him, and Jesus's acknowledging his leadership and precedence, shows that the Mahdi is in a station of divine guidance and elevation that makes the Ummah commanded to follow him without""")

t(38, 60, """discussion. Whoever Allah leads to lead a prophet in prayer has no need of correction from the common people.

3. The text on the descent of comprehensive cosmic blessings in his era
* The text: From Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: "My Ummah will enjoy in his time a bounty the like of which they have never enjoyed; the sky will send abundant rain upon them, and the earth will not withhold any of its plants" (narrated by al-Tabarani and Ahmad, and graded sound by al-Hakim).
* Indication and particularity: This harmony between the movement of the cosmos (sky and earth) and his leadership indicates that he is a servant whose obedience has been made a cause of absolute blessing. This success and comprehensive justice that changes the face of the earth prove in practice that his management is guided by revelation and divine help, which removes the need to correct or oppose him. And revelation to other than prophets is established in many ways other than the revelation that came to prophets for conveying the messages..

Except that there is no prophet nor messenger after our Prophet ﷺ, and none disputes this firm truth but an ignorant person of tribulation.

- The text on the certainty of his victory and the angels' support for him

* The text: It has come in narrations about his sending and emergence that he will come out with an angel with him, or an angel above his head calling out: "This is the Mahdi, the Caliph of Allah, so follow him" (reported by Abu Nu'aym in Akhbar al-Mahdi, graded sound by al-Bazzar and al-Ruyani, and it has supporting witnesses).
* Indication and particularity: The direct heavenly call from the angels appointing him and describing him as "the Caliph of Allah" lifts from his rule any ordinary human character. When the appointment and support are heavenly and public to the Ummah, people's right to argue or correct falls away accordingly, and obedience becomes a doctrinal duty in compliance with the command of heaven.

- The text on practical infallibility in performance (he leaves no Sunnah without establishing it)

* The text: From Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, describing his comprehensive justice and his establishing of the religion: "He will fill the earth with equity and justice as it was filled with oppression and injustice... he will abolish the jizyah, and call for wealth, and no one will accept it",

And in the sound reports from Ibn Sirin: "The Mahdi is from this Ummah, and he is the one who will lead Jesus son of Mary in prayer", and he used to say: (He is almost preferred over some of the prophets). (Narrated by Ibn Abi Shaybah in al-Musannaf with a sound chain.)

From Ibn Sirin's saying is understood a preference in terms of the particularity and cosmic mission of the end times, not an absolute preference, and no sane person doubts this; it is part of thinking well of a great scholar such as Ibn Sirin, may Allah the Exalted have mercy on him.

* Indication and particularity: The ruler who succeeds in erasing oppression and injustice completely and absolutely in practice from the face of the whole earth is a ruler who has reached the peak of right guidance and divine help. Humans err and get it right; they act justly in one place and unjustly in another, whereas the divine generalization (he will fill the earth with equity and justice) is a certificate of right guidance that spares the subjects the need to correct him.

The text on awe and immediate empowerment (the pledge of the reluctant one, to whom all submit)

The text on the army that seeks to fight him being swallowed up (absolute divine protection)

* The text: The continuation of the hadith of Umm Salamah in Sahih Muslim and Abu Dawud: "...An army from the Levant will be sent against him, and they will be swallowed up in the desert between Makkah and Madinah; when people see that, the Abdal of the Levant and groups of the people of Iraq will come to him and pledge allegiance to him..."

* Indication and particularity: The intervention of divine decree by directly and openly swallowing up the army that wants to fight him is a decisive "cosmic sign" for the Ummah. This swallowing cuts doubt with certainty and makes clear to all people that this man is protected by Allah's protection, and that Allah fights on his behalf and defends him by swallowing up an entire army; it is not permissible for his subjects and supporters to do anything but obey his management and submit fully to his command.

These texts and others clearly prove the divine particularity, with which it is not right that this figure should come out in that time and say words contradicting these sound hadiths.""")

h(38, 2400, """The second universality of Islam in the end times is a divine resurrection, and not by the measures of the first universality""")

t(39, 690, """Since I wanted good for Muslims in general by explaining the importance of revering the name of Allah the Almighty on hearing it, the case of some who are ignorant of Allah the Exalted, whose hearts, the seat of reasoning and understanding, have been closed upon them, was that they thought in their ignorance that they had triumphed, and they did not grasp the reality of the position they had come from.

Our Prophet ﷺ did not lie down except in rare cases, and because he is a prophet through whom Allah the Exalted legislated for people, unlike us, members of his Ummah, through whom Allah the Exalted legislates nothing, it was among his particularities ﷺ that he was the first to apply the legislation, in explanation, approval, conduct and word. And since people are strong and weak, healthy and sick, so that this test might be lifted from the Ummah, as weak bodies are compelled to lie down to rest the bodies while desiring remembrance in this state, it is natural that the Sunnah of the Prophet ﷺ should contain an explanation of the permissibility of an act for those who need it, and an explanation that this act is not forbidden.

Among what these ignorant ones cited is the hadith of the Mother of the Believers Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, about the Prophet ﷺ lying down in her lap while she was menstruating. I said in an earlier post that the reason for this hadith, in which the Prophet ﷺ did this, is to correct people's understanding that a woman is not impure when she menstruates, to remove the legacy that prevailed in society's thinking before Islam; it also contains the permissibility of reading the Book of Allah the Exalted lying down for a reason, not merely for one's mood. But these ignorant ones made the matter a Sunnah to be followed and keenly practiced, and made it a habit of a person when he wants to read the Book of Allah, or they took this permissibility as evidence, in the illusions of their minds, that revering the name of Allah the Exalted is not a virtue, saying: Do it yourself and do not impose it on us.

First: O ignorant ones, I did not advise it by telling people that whoever does not do it is sinful; I only offered advice on revering the name of Allah the Exalted as a way of increasing the servant's esteem for his Lord, and it is something the Prophet ﷺ did every day of the year in the years of his life; he ﷺ sat upright in 99% of his states, so why do you not imitate him? Will your bellies, which stretch two and a half meters forward, enable you to sit with straight backs all the time, as the Prophet ﷺ did every day of his life?

The answer is no. The two and a half meters of belly, I know, leave you sprawled flat on the ground all day.

You dived into Bukhari and Muslim and extracted something the Prophet ﷺ did once, and presented it as permission to do it much and often, while the Prophet ﷺ did not do it much. So why do you want this act, which the Prophet ﷺ did once, as a Sunnah we do every day, and take the permissibility of that noble Prophetic act as an entry point, in the illusion of your minds, to reject the call to sit upright on hearing the name of Allah?

I accept from you, O people of bellies, flabbiness, gluttony and lying flat on the ground all day, that you walk the path of the Prophet ﷺ, so that one of you reads the Qur'an lying down once; there is no harm in that, as the Prophet ﷺ did. But as for adhering to the Sunnah of the Prophet ﷺ, which is that he sat with a straight back every day, why do you want the once-only Sunnah in the hadith of the Mother of the Believers Aisha, and reject hundreds of hadiths that in his daily life he sat with a straight back, not lying down?

Thus did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ revere his Lord, in that in his daily life he did not lie down at all, O you whose bellies stretch forward. So why should he need to straighten his sitting at all, when he was upright in 99% of his states?!

As for our Lord's word about remembering Him standing, sitting and on their sides, I have already set out the scholars' words that it concerns the remembrances of sleep, the remembrances of waking in the middle of sleep, and the concession for the sick.""")

t(40, 60, """You do not like to walk according to his Sunnah, and you believe that the way out for your bellies is the hadith of Lady Aisha. It is not a way out, and you have no way out with me at all. You will not manage it, by the power of Allah the Exalted .. many have tried before you.

The Prophet ﷺ sat daily with a straight back, in a proper sitting, and this is the truth .. And the hadith of his lying down is sound, but it represents a single situation and not a daily Sunnah of life. But because of your bellies, you want to use the permissibility in the hadith of the Mother of the Believers Aisha to tell people that the Prophet lay down all day as we lie down with our bellies! Where will you go?!
-------------------------
A subtle warning has come from the great Companion Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, that clarifies the difference between lying down for rest and remembrance, and lying down out of laziness and idleness, as the people of bellies do.
• It is narrated that he said: "I dislike that a man should read the Qur'an while stretching out." "Stretching out" is sprawling and swaggering while lying down, accompanied by yawning and laziness, because it is contrary to reverence.

2. The condition that reading be for worship and seeking, not for amusement (Ibrahim al-Nakha'i)

It is reported from Imam Ibrahim al-Nakha'i (one of the great jurists of the Successors in Kufa) that he distinguished between two cases:
• He saw no harm in reading while lying down if the reader seeks reward, or is revising what he memorized, or reads his daily portions before sleep.
• But he disliked that a person make lying down a permanent habit for reading, as a pastime, or as if he were reading an ordinary book for entertainment, without reflection and humility of heart.""")

h(40, 1200, """The connection between the huge belly and these people's understanding of our Lord's word: “Those who remember Allah standing, sitting and on their sides”""")

t(40, 1890, """A fierce satanic campaign from companions who incite them intensely!

1- I did not say "obligatory" in the sense of "legislation", nor in the sense of "making lawful and forbidden"; I only said it is obligatory in the sense of "advising respect for the name of Allah the Exalted", as a father says to his son: you must respect people. But these do not understand speech.
2- I never said that whoever does not do it commits a sin or a major sin, but these do not understand speech.
3- I advised and insisted on doing it "by way of admonition", by way of explanation, and by way of advice (((((out of love))))) for people to earn reward. I did not say the matter is obligatory by a text; rather I said it is obligatory by understanding the texts, for whoever grasps and wants to revere Allah the Exalted. I did not say that whoever does not do it commits a sin or a major sin. But they do not love those who advise them, nor those who advise them what benefits them and strengthens their relationship with Allah the Exalted; they only love those who lead them astray.

To those for whom respecting the name of Allah the Exalted is hard, I say: I ask Allah the Almighty to bear down on your hearts more and more. O Allah, amen.

As for citing the hadith of the Mother of the Believers Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, about the Prophet ﷺ reading the Qur'an in her lap while she was menstruating, it is a hadith of legislation, not a hadith of a binding, obligatory Sunnah to be followed and always keenly practiced, with the hadith taken as a concession to lie down and read the Book of Allah, and that considered a Sunnah whose practice is preferred over reading while sitting. It is a hadith of legislation, and of the permissibility that the Muslim woman in a state of menstruation is not""")

t(41, 60, """impure, as people looked at the woman before Islam. As for the hadith establishing a Sunnah to be followed, then these people must wait for their wives to menstruate and then deliberately read the Qur'an in their laps, as long as they think the matter is an obligatory Sunnah to be followed and keenly practiced. These people do not understand the difference between the permissibility of such an act for a reason, even once in a lifetime, out of kind treatment of the wife, and the Sunnah that is followed, and considering this act in their minds as always obligatory and a fixed principle in the manner of reading the Qur'an. Thus did these ignorant ones understand the act of the Prophet ﷺ, and they viewed this permissibility as contradicting what I called for.

We said: Come, let us revere Allah; we did not say that any act of our Prophet's acts was a mistake. And I explained the verse they did not understand in an earlier post. Then they brought the Prophet's ﷺ reading in the lap of Lady Aisha while she was menstruating. Here I reply: my call was not a prohibition or a legislation; I only learned that the Prophet ﷺ in his waking hours was sitting, not lying down, so I knew he sanctified the name of Allah, and I wanted us to do as he did. But the devils are not pleased!

Accordingly .. this is a post of excuse to our Almighty Lord by way of explanation, not of busying myself with the ignorance of the ignorant, for I am never accustomed to replying, I do not care about replying, and I find no value in a reply with hearts that do not understand speech, do not grasp meaning and do not see light.""")

h(41, 1000, """Revering the name of Allah and the objection of the objectors: these people still bring verses and hadiths to say that advising people to revere the name of Allah creates hardship for people!""")

t(41, 1690, """The call to care for sanctifying Allah the Exalted still meets resistance from some souls, as if the call were a call to misguidance. But here I will set out, explaining the meaning, the verse that whoever dislikes the advice on the necessity of revering Allah the Exalted cites out of ignorance, and I know the matter root and branch; it is nothing more than a locked heart closed upon itself.

In the word of our Almighty Lord, holy are His names and attributes, exalted in the heavens and on earth, and High, greatly exalted:

"Those who remember Allah standing, sitting and on their sides"

Lying on one's side is the natural posture of a person in sleep or illness, and the sick are excused; therefore many scholars and exegetes linked this verse with the remembrances of sleep, waking and turning over in bed.
This understanding is supported and confirmed by two basic matters in the Prophetic Sunnah:
1. The practical application of the Prophet ﷺ (the Sunnah of lying down)
When the Prophet ﷺ went to his bed, he would actually translate this verse by lying on his right side, then begin remembering Allah and reading the Qur'an:
* He reads Ayat al-Kursi and the last two verses of Surah al-Baqarah.
* He joins his palms, blows into them, reads al-Ikhlas and al-Mu'awwidhatayn, and wipes his body with them.
* He says his well-known remembrances, such as: "In Your name, my Lord, I lay down my side, and by You I raise it..." and "O Allah, I have submitted myself to You..."

The verse described the state of the sincere believers: even when they are in their beds preparing for sleep (on their sides), they are not heedless of their Lord, but make the last of their words in this world His remembrance, Glorified is He.""")

t(42, 80, """2. Remembrance when turning over at night
The meaning also extends to what happens during sleep; when the believer wakes suddenly at night and turns from side to side (and on their sides), the remembrance of Allah runs spontaneously on his tongue from the intensity of his heart's attachment to Him.
The Prophet ﷺ said about this: "Whoever wakes at night (that is, wakes and turns over) and says: There is no god but Allah alone, with no partner... then supplicates, it will be answered for him."
Conclusion
The remembrances of sleep are the most prominent and daily application of His word, Exalted is He: "and on their sides". The verse came to cover all states:
* Standing: while you strive for your living, or pray.
* Sitting: while you are in your gathering, or reading, or resting seated, that is, sitting in a proper posture.
* On their sides: while you are in your bed preparing for sleep, or turning over at night, or sick with the excuse of illness.

A little resistance to the companion who will lead you to the watering places of ruin""")

h(42, 900, """The meaning of the word of the One whose names and attributes are holy: “Those who remember Allah standing, sitting and on their sides”""")

t(42, 1590, """The natural state and daily routine of the Prophet ﷺ was activity, movement, sitting (cross-legged or squatting) or standing. He led military campaigns, received delegations, addressed people, judged between disputants, and walked in the markets to teach people and meet their needs.
As for what came in some hadiths that he sometimes reclined or lay down in the mosque, that was in (((very rare cases))) due to severe fatigue or to clarify a particular matter, and it was not his habit nor the greater part of his time.

That is why it is not reported of him ﷺ that he straightened his sitting when the name of Allah the Exalted was mentioned, because he was basically upright in his sitting, so how would he straighten up?""")

h(42, 2300, """The Prophet sat in a proper posture and rarely lay down, and the call to sit upright on hearing the name of Allah the Almighty is for an Ummah that has come to lie down more than it sits upright""")

t(43, 470, """He wrote a long, broad screed objecting and considering the reverence of Allah the Almighty, our Lord, by sitting upright an evil innovation, a work of Satan, misguidance, and an addition to the religion that contradicts the Sharia and the commands of Allah the Exalted!""")

h(43, 900, """A commenter refused to accept sitting upright when he hears the name of Allah. Can I do anything for you if you are this blind of insight?""")

h(43, 2000, """Qutuz lived for a time unable to define his goal in life, until he saw a dream! A dream that made history""")

h(44, 450, """Osman son of Ertugrul lived for a time unable to define his goal in life, until he saw a dream! A dream that made history""")

h(44, 1500, """While Hulagu was laughing as he killed the last Abbasid caliph by kicking, women were gathering around a mother giving birth to a child named Osman son of Ertugrul""")

t(44, 2170, """It is time to reveal this gift coming, God willing, to the Islamic world .. it is not an opinion nor a guess, God willing

There will be the second universality, and people, by the power of Allah the Exalted, will see a face very closely resembling, though not 100% identical to, the face of the Prophet ﷺ. ((And Allah the Exalted alone knows best)) how great the resemblance will be; it is something we will all see only in its time and moment, and Allah knows best. Though resemblance certainly does not mean being identical, since the Prophet ﷺ used the word "he resembles me", what if the resemblance were 99%? Allah alone knows best the proportion we will see. As for 100%, it would have been understood through another wording, such as "as if his face were cut from my face", a wording the Prophet ﷺ did not say.

And it is important that I say: if the resemblance were slight, it would not have deserved mention by the Prophet ﷺ, and this confirms, by Allah the Exalted's leave, that the resemblance is very great, not merely great, and I would not be surprised if it reached 90% to 99%.""")

h(45, 500, """Resemblance in physical form, not in character. No one can resemble the Prophet in his character""")

h(45, 1500, """The industrial revolution is a Western precedence and a real superiority, but it cannot dominate in the time of the kingdom of heaven""")

h(45, 2350, """Western intervention in the world's affairs is a deep-rooted Western practice that made …""")
